A||||8||Zman302||roof skin replacement||||5||
Z||000000||Zman302||07-02-2000||12:17 AM||||I've got a chance at getting a factory sunroof setup cheap and was wondering about getting the roof skin off.  From the looks of things it looks like the windshield needs to come out but i've been told it doesn't need to. If anyone has any info on this i would greatly appreciate it.  <P>                Thanks, Craig||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||07-02-2000||07:46 AM||||I have done the swap on a friends car. You do not need to remove the windsheild.<BR>The only things you need to remove is the plastic vent panel and wiper arms from the outside and the headliner on the inside. Some cars you may have to remove the black rubber trim around the windshield. (It depends on if the windshield was ever replaced.) There are 2 bolts on the inside and 2 or 4 on the outside. The hardest part about the swap is the area on the front part of the roof just above the windsheild has a black tar type of sealant on it. In order to remove the roof you need to cut thru the sealant. We used an 18" X 1" piece of sheet metal with a duct tape handle. It doesn't need to be sharpe but sturdy and long. Starting from the driver side slide it between the panel and the roof and slowly push it along. Be very careful so you don't chip the edge of the windsheild. Takes awhile to get through this stuff. Also the corners where the roof goes down by the side of the windsheid is the weakest spot on the roof. If your saving the old roof extreme caution must be used. Putting on the old one is easy. Just peel the tar stuff from old roof and put on the new roof. You still need to watch the weak corners.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||07-02-2000||08:22 AM||||There is a step-by-step how-to here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-02-2000||09:53 AM||||After reading all the info I wondered if it would be easier to use an old guitar string, or some piano wire to cut the roof adhesive, anyone ever try that?<P><BR>Taiji<P>modified 85 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||07-02-2000||11:36 AM||||Ive done the sunroof swap. its pretty straight forward. If you have any  questions or problems. I can help also. Yes a guitar string or similiar works good. Also be carful when removing the old roof. The part that wraps down around the bottom of the windshield has a tendency to catch the glass and chip it. I know I have a big chip in the bottom of mine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TOM||07-02-2000||11:43 AM||||also you will need to get the mounts under the hood to hold the glass panel when not on the roof. And you might need to get the small windshield washer tank as they are different.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zman302||07-03-2000||11:15 PM||||thought i would say thanks, your info has made my decision easy.  Friday i will go to the yard and pull the parts i need.  <P>              Thanks again, Craig<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||07-03-2000||11:48 PM||||There's also a plastic deflector, that came with the original, that goes in the front of the hole to deflect wind from comming in the cockpit. You might look for that in the doner, it clips in the front trunk. Although that's probably too much to ask for.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TOM||07-04-2000||12:51 AM||||I have a stash of the wind deflector things if you dont find one. ||||reg||1
